I’ll first inventory the pattern, metadata, and reusable refinement utilities, then identify plausible phases from chemistry and peak positions. I’ll keep phase admission residual-led and will verify the final exports against a reopened GSAS-II project.

The chemistry strongly suggests scheelite-type SrWO₄ as the primary product, but I’m treating precursor remnants and competing Sr–W–O compounds as open hypotheses. Next I’m extracting the peak inventory and querying the local crystallographic database for exact and physically plausible records before any refinement.

The dominant pattern is unequivocally ambient scheelite SrWO₄: its full reflection sequence from 18° through 95° is present, while the high-pressure monoclinic SrWO₄ record is incompatible. Several coherent weak families around 22–25°, 34–44°, and 50–65° remain unexplained, so I’m now testing residual phases rather than forcing them into background/profile terms.

I’m building a one-phase GSAS-II baseline with the measured Cu Kα doublet, Bragg–Brentano geometry, a smooth low-order background, one specimen-displacement correction, and a nonnegative U/W/X profile. This baseline is deliberately free of size, strain, texture, roughness, and atomic-coordinate refinement so the residual can be interpreted physically.

The baseline exposed two important facts: the strongest unexplained peak at 29.754° is distinct from SrWO₄(004) at 30.22°, and a matching weak family continues at 24.73°, 32.60°, 34.50°, 44.65°, and 48.61°. I also rejected the first profile trial because U refined negative; I’m returning to a bounded physical baseline and testing chemically plausible secondary structures, including analogue-derived Sr-rich prototypes where the exact database is sparse.

The corrected residual positions confirm that the 23.47/23.97/24.73° triplet is consistent with a WO₃ polymorph, while the very strong 29.754° line still needs a separate explanation. I’m ranking exact WO₃/Sr–W–O records and substituted Sr-rich prototypes under the same displacement/profile model; candidates are being judged by coherent multi-line improvement, not a single coincident peak.

The exact-record challenge is underway with each candidate in its own recoverable GSAS-II project. The ambient monoclinic WO₃ records are the leading position/intensity matches; high-pressure SrWO₄ is being retained only as a falsification test because its source conditions are incompatible with the synthesis.

Ambient monoclinic WO₃ is now supported by a positive, stable scale and a full triplet/multi-family match; the two equivalent monoclinic records converge to essentially the same cell and Rwp. Texture trials only improve Rwp by ~0.03 percentage points, so texture is not being used as a surrogate for the remaining peaks. I’m now testing isovalent Sr-rich prototypes and the exact minor-phase watch list against the accepted two-phase baseline.

The watch-list challenge has now separated real structure support from scale artifacts. Isovalent Sr₃WO₆ improves Rwp to 18.29%, but exact Sr₃W₂O₉ improves it much more—to 16.87%—with a stable positive scale and a plausible ~3% cell-volume adjustment. SrO and high-pressure monoclinic SrWO₄ give only small improvements and lack coherent unique-family support; they remain rejected. I’m checking whether Sr₃W₂O₉ accounts for multiple isolated families and whether Sr₃WO₆ adds anything independently.

The provisional inventory is now four phases: scheelite SrWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, Sr₃W₂O₉, and a tentative Sr₃WO₆ prototype. Each minor phase has positive scale and support from multiple peak families; the Sr₃WO₆ assignment remains the least certain because it relies on an isovalent prototype. I’m now stress-testing the common profile, size/strain terms, and replay stability before locking fractions and exports.

The common W+X width model is justified: W-only and X-only each worsen Rwp by 0.49–0.97 points. Free SH/L is unsupported (it turns negative and is strongly correlated with displacement), so asymmetry stays fixed. A free polarization term gives an apparently large improvement only by running to 1.27, outside its physical 0–1 domain; I’m treating that as a warning about intensity-model bias, not as an admissible fit parameter, and will bracket it with physically bounded trials.

The intensity trend is now bracketed. Polarization alone wants an impossible value (>1); a two-parameter roughness model is singular and drives one coefficient negative. A one-parameter Suortti roughness correction is positive and replay-stable with the standard unpolarized factor fixed at 0.5, so that is the cleanest admissible baseline. I’m retaining only that single roughness term and will now re-test texture and the phase watch list against it.

The Sr₃WO₆ watch-list model was genuinely source-sensitive. Replacing the P2₁/c analogue with an independent Sr₃UO₆-derived P2₁ polymorph lowers Rwp from 13.13% to 12.96%, with positive fixed-cell support and stable replay. Its inherited W–O geometry is only approximate, however, so I will label this phase and its fraction as tentative rather than present the prototype as uniquely determined. SrO also survives the clean-profile challenge at about seven scale standard deviations and is now included as a weak fifth phase.

The final broadening challenge supports two additional terms, both physically signed and independent: about 0.47% isotropic microstrain for Sr₃W₂O₉ and 0.48% for the tentative Sr₃WO₆-like phase. Each is significant (>4σ), replay-stable, and not strongly correlated with the other. Size broadening for SrO collapses to an implausible ~5 nm while its scale inflates eightfold, so I rejected that as a background surrogate.

The final nuisance model is now fixed: eight smooth Chebyshev terms (six terms worsened Rwp by 0.23 points), W+X common width terms, one specimen-displacement correction, one positive roughness term, no texture, and no SH/L refinement. A seeded zero-offset alternative converged but fit worse and gave less plausible cells, so specimen displacement is retained. I’m locking the project and exporting all deliverables from that exact replayed state.

Completed the five-phase GSAS-II refinement.

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| SrWO₄, scheelite | 70.24(34)% |
| Monoclinic WO₃ | 11.01(23)% |
| Sr₃W₂O₉ | 10.72(28)% |
| SrO | 0.49(10)% |
| Sr₃WO₆-like, tentative | 7.54(33)% |

Final fit: Rwp 12.897%, Rp 9.111%, GOF 3.656. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases only.

The Sr₃WO₆ structure is not uniquely determined; plausible prototypes place it around 5–8 wt%. Sr₃W₂O₉ is moderately confident, while SrO is a low-confidence trace assignment.

